[entry]
sig_id: 2101411
sig_msg: GPL SNMP public access udp
category: None
explanation: This alert indicates that a device is attempting to query network equipment using SNMP with the default “public” / "private" community string. In Simple Network Management Protocol, “public” is a well-known default that often allows read-only access to device information. While not an exploit by itself, this activity is commonly associated with reconnaissance. Attackers frequently use default SNMP strings to gather details about network devices (e.g., system info, interfaces, routing data). it may indicate someone probing your network for information, especially if the source is unfamiliar.
tags: 
refs: 
---

[entry]
sig_id: 2027397
sig_msg: ET INFO Spotify P2P Client
category: None
explanation: This alert indicates that a device on the network is using Spotify, which in some configurations can use peer-to-peer connections to stream or share cached music data between clients. However, because it involves P2P communication: It may bypass strict network controls It can consume bandwidth In rare cases, it may expose internal IP information externally
tags: 
refs: 
---

[entry]
sig_id: 2210029
sig_msg: SURICATA STREAM ESTABLISHED invalid ack
category: None
explanation: This alert means that a TCP packet acknowledged data in a way that doesn’t match the expected session state. In simple terms, something about the connection flow looked out of sync or malformed. Most of the time, this is not an attack. Common benign causes include: Packet loss or network instability Out-of-order packets Asymmetric routing (traffic taking different paths) Firewall/NAT devices altering traffic In some cases it can be linked to: Network scanning or probing tools Evasion techniques attempting to confuse IDS/IPS tracking Misconfigured or faulty network devices
tags: 
refs: 
---

[entry]
sig_id: 2210045
sig_msg: SURICATA STREAM Packet with invalid ack
category: None
explanation: This alert indicates that a TCP packet contains an acknowledgment number that does not align with the expected state of the connection. In short, the packet doesn’t “make sense” based on what’s been seen so far in that session.
tags: 
refs: 
---

[entry]
sig_id: 2013504
sig_msg: ET INFO GNU/Linux APT User-Agent Outbound likely related to package management
category: None
explanation: This alert indicates that a system on the network is using a Linux package manager (commonly apt on Debian/Ubuntu systems) to check for updates or download software packages. Assessment: This is normal and expected behavior for Linux systems performing: System updates (apt update, apt upgrade) Package installations or security patching It is generally benign and even desirable from a security standpoint, since it suggests systems may be staying up to date. When to look closer: The traffic originates from a device that should not be running Linux The destination repositories are unknown or untrusted Activity occurs at unusual times or in abnormal volumes
tags: 
refs: 
---

[entry]
sig_id: 2210010
sig_msg: SURICATA STREAM 3way handshake wrong seq wrong ack
category: None
explanation: This alert means the initial TCP connection setup (SYN → SYN/ACK → ACK) did not follow expected sequence/acknowledgment values. In simple terms, the connection establishment looked inconsistent or malformed.
tags: 
refs: 
---

[entry]
sig_id: 2033078
sig_msg: ET INFO Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN Binding Request On Non-Standard High Port)
category: None
explanation: This alert indicates that a device on the network is attempting to discover its public IP address and NAT behavior using STUN, but doing so over an unusual (non-standard) port. This is typically benign and expected in modern networks. STUN is widely used by: VoIP applications (e.g., softphones) Video conferencing tools (Zoom, Teams, WebRTC apps) Real-time communication services
tags: 
refs: 
---

